<p>Testing Electrical and Mechanical Items</p>
<ul style="list-style: none; padding-left: 0; margin: 18px 0;">
<li style="margin-bottom: 10px; display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 10px;"><i class="fas fa-check-circle" style="color: #10b981; margin-top: 5px; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 0.95rem;"></i><span>All electrical appliances and mechanical fixtures are powered on in a dedicated test zone to confirm functionality and safety compliance.</span></li>
<li style="margin-bottom: 10px; display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 10px;"><i class="fas fa-check-circle" style="color: #10b981; margin-top: 5px; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 0.95rem;"></i><span>Voltage, current, and load tests are recorded on a standardized form, and any faulty units are isolated for repair or replacement.</span></li>
<li style="margin-bottom: 10px; display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 10px;"><i class="fas fa-check-circle" style="color: #10b981; margin-top: 5px; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 0.95rem;"></i><span>Mechanical items such as HVAC units, elevators, and conveyor belts undergo operational checks, with technicians verifying torque settings and safety interlocks.</span></li>
